<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Are water-based paints and adhesives completely safe for hearing?</h3>
<p>They’re generally lower in volatile organic solvents than solvent-based products, which is helpful. But “water-based” doesn’t equal zero risk. Some still contain co-solvents or other ototoxicants, and you’ll still have sanding noise, compressors, and dryers to contend with. Keep ventilation, proper respirators, and hearing protection in the plan.</p>

<h3>Does carbon monoxide by itself cause hearing loss?</h3>
<p>Carbon monoxide reduces oxygen delivery to tissues. The cochlea is metabolically hungry, so hypoxia can stress it. CO exposure is particularly concerning when combined with noise, which raises injury risk. Always prevent CO buildup—vent engines and heaters outdoors and use CO detectors. If a detector alarms, leave the area and seek fresh air.</p>

<h3>Will antioxidant supplements protect my hearing if I’m exposed to solvents and noise?</h3>
<p>There’s no proven over-the-counter supplement that prevents chemical + noise-related hearing loss. Some antioxidants (like N‑acetylcysteine) have been studied with mixed, inconclusive results. Your best evidence-based strategy is controlling exposures, using the right respirator and well-fitted hearing protection, and getting regular hearing checks. Talk with your healthcare provider before starting any supplement, especially at high doses.</p>

<h3>I already wear earplugs. Do I really need earmuffs too?</h3>
<p>If you face very loud or impulse noise—especially alongside solvent fumes—adding earmuffs over well-inserted plugs can provide an extra 5–10 dB of real-world protection. It’s a small change that can make a big difference over years of exposure. Ensure both are fitted correctly; poor fit kills performance.</p>


